{"agent":"lane-lock","data":{"by":"grok-4.7","evidence":{"chunk_findings":[{"chunk":1,"detail":"`renewer` only returns a callable and merge invokes it twice, both before the blocking `W.merge`. Nothing renews on an interval while that call runs, and nothing is stopped when it returns. With a short lease TTL, a merge that takes longer than the TTL (the 10-minute case in the acceptance) lets the lease expire mid-merge; a waiter can take the item or the holder loses it, which is the failure this change was supposed to close. The new test only counts that the spy lambda was called at t=0, so it stays green when the lease dies during `W.merge`.","digest":"909e63e2a1349384","family":"xai","location":"ddflow/api/lifecycle/merge.py:315","malformed":"","reviewer":"grok-4.7","severity":"HIGH","title":"ddflow/api/lifecycle/merge.py:315"},{"chunk":1,"detail":"`_no_renewal` succeeds only when `gone.state` is `DONE` or `ABANDONED`. A released item is neither: claim then release, then `heartbeat` on that id. Control falls through to `L.renew`, which is the old failure (`no lease held`, exit non-zero) or, if a stale lease record is still live, an extension. The contract requires that case to return success and not touch the lease. The new test never releases an item, so it does not catch this.","digest":"be5ca485b2a7e032","family":"xai","location":"ddflow/api/lifecycle/heartbeat.py:92","malformed":"","reviewer":"grok-4.7","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/api/lifecycle/heartbeat.py:92"},{"chunk":1,"detail":"The accepted behavior also requires `doctor` to list heartbeat-looking processes whose item is already finished (pid and command line) and the pkill hook to say how to stop them. This change never touches doctor or that hook. After a finished item, a leftover heartbeat process is invisible to `doctor`, so the operator has no listing to act on.","digest":"708d2a4c37242a71","family":"xai","location":"ddflow/services/leasekeep.py:41","malformed":"","reviewer":"grok-4.7","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/services/leasekeep.py:41"},{"chunk":2,"detail":"The heartbeat scanner treats a separate flag value as the item id. `_HEARTBEAT` only skips `-\\S+` tokens, so `ddflow heartbeat --agent worker T1` (or the same command inside `while true; do ...; done`) captures `worker`, not `T1`. With finished `[\"T1\"]`, `m.group(1) not in done` drops the process and `doctor` never lists pid or command line. The new test only uses `--agent` before the subcommand (`ddflow --agent a heartbeat T1`), so it does not catch this. `ddflow heartbeat --agent=worker T1` still works, which shows the gap is valued options the pattern claims to skip.","digest":"828853a14ed9c2bc","family":"xai","location":"ddflow/services/orphan_suites.py:188","malformed":"","reviewer":"grok-4.7","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/services/orphan_suites.py:188"},{"chunk":2,"detail":"`_HEARTBEAT.search` keeps only the first hit. A leftover loop `while true; do ddflow heartbeat T-live; ddflow heartbeat T1; sleep 60; done` with finished `[\"T1\"]` matches `T-live`, fails the `done` check, and is omitted even though it still heartbeats a finished item. `finditer` over every `ddflow heartbeat` item would list pid 4001; this path returns nothing.","digest":"ad42872c3cb063ba","family":"xai","location":"ddflow/services/orphan_suites.py:211","malformed":"","reviewer":"grok-4.7","severity":"LOW","title":"ddflow/services/orphan_suites.py:211"}],"chunks_total":2,"coverage":"2/2 chunk(s) reviewed","delta_findings":2,"delta_from":"6f1a210e43a9b434dd002d4ef567c806a0e4abcb","delta_raised":["828853a14ed9c2bc","ad42872c3cb063ba"],"delta_rounds":1,"diff_chars":12111,"diff_sha":"8f4591f40e7115466ba961ed8bb59a2ef429cf7d3dfb7062705c340650af551c","diff_source":"delta review of uncommitted changes since 6f1a210e43","diff_stat":{"deletions":11,"files":8,"insertions":138,"untracked":0},"elapsed_s":128.4,"family":"xai","findings":5,"full_coverage":"1/1 chunk(s) reviewed","max_chunk_chars":8000,"model":"grok-4.7",